/// A user's Gmail labels changed for an assistant, as the native tool
/// `GmailModifyLabels`, answered by `api-request` calls against the
/// `oauth_provider` the instance names, stamped with the calling
/// conversation's owner. The user's token never reaches this component;
/// `api-request` attaches it host-side. Every call is nested under a request:
/// an instance whose clauses say `user = "*"` cannot call on its own account,
/// so a caller with no user of its own is answered `denied`. Its one call is
/// Gmail's `messages/batchModify`, under the Gmail organize scope set.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// host, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tool exist; `log` records each
/// step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a query.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `gmail_host` is the `host[:port]` Gmail is called
/// on, which `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts` and
/// the instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails every
/// activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up
/// to a power of two. This class's Σ is 2, which the family default of 16
/// holds; the block in the guide restates it and sets `noise = alarm`.
component GoogleGmailOrganize {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide gmail-modify-labels {
    mcp_name = "GmailModifyLabels";
    description = "Add labels to and remove labels from Gmail messages, in one change Google applies to every message or to none. `ids` is a list of 1 to 1000 distinct message `id`s from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`; the ids together may hold at most 48000 characters, and more is refused: ask for fewer. `add` and `remove` are each a list of 1 to 20 distinct label ids as `GmailLabels` lists them (`INBOX`, `UNREAD`, `Label_12`), never label names: at least one of them must be given, and no label may be in both. Removing `INBOX` archives a message; removing `UNREAD` marks it read. `add` may not name `TRASH` or `SPAM`: these tools never move mail to Trash or Spam, and such a change is refused; `remove` may name them. Answers `accepted: true`, `ids` (how many messages) and `add` and `remove` as asked: Google accepted the change for every message. A label or message id Google does not know is refused with Google's message, and nothing was changed. The change is idempotent: a refusal saying the labels may already have been changed means Google may have applied it before its answer was lost, and asking again with the same arguments is safe. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail organize scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "ids": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 1000, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "add": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 20, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "remove": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 20, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" } }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["ids"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
}
